Generated operation description:
1. Convert all the time strings in the 'act_dep_time' column into a uniform format (e.g., ISO 8601 with Z time zone). This will make it easier to compare and analyze the data.

   Example:
   - row 17: `1900-03-31T17:08:00Z` (already in correct format)
   - row 19: `1900-03-31T20:25:00Z`
   - row 24: `1900-03-31T14:26:00Z`
   - ... and so on

   Operation: Replace the existing 'act_dep_time' column with a new column containing ISO 8601 formatted time strings.

   SQL Query:
   ```sql
   ALTER TABLE table_name ADD act_dep_time_iso8601 AS (
      CASE
         WHEN act_dep_time LIKE '% pm' THEN CONCAT(SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 1, 4), '-', SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 6, 2), '-', SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 9, 2), ' ', SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 12, 2), ':', SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 15, 2), ':00')
         WHEN act_dep_time LIKE '% am' THEN CONCAT(SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 1, 4), '-', SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 6, 2), '-', SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 9, 2), ' ', SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 12, 2) - 12, ':', SUBSTRING(act_dep_time, 15, 2), ':00')
         ELSE act_dep_time
      END
   );
   ```

   Note that the above query assumes that all time strings are in 12-hour format (either am or pm). If there are any 24-hour format time strings, you will need to adjust the calculation accordingly.

   After applying this operation, it will be easier to identify delayed schedules as the scheduled departure times can also be converted into a uniform format for comparison.

   Additionally, you may want to add another column to store the scheduled departure times if they are not already available in your dataset. This will help in calculating the delay more accurately.
